FDT as the essay interprets it, with two copies that see different observations, x from Rowan's lead and y from Sasha's. I'm wondering whether intervening on one copy's decision node would change the other's act, or whether that depends on a subjunctive edge, and I'm checking what the essay's procedure says about the edges from D_x.\n\nI'm checking whether the other prisoner's act actually depends on my decision node, since they compute their response to y and not x. If there's no subjunctive edge, A-FDT just gives a best response, and defection dominates. But section 6.2 raises policy-FDT as a deferral option, and I'm trying to pin down whether that applies here, given the essay's wording about self-consistent assignments.\n\nI'm weighing whether the strict A-FDT reading should win here, since the other copy's act comes from y rather than x, and intervening on D_x alone wouldn't reach it without a subjunctive edge. The alternative is that the two copies' outputs are logically linked by symmetry, but the essay's intervention rule doesn't propagate that link on its own.\n\nI'm noting that the essay explicitly keeps D_R fixed when D_L moves, so the one-input intervention doesn't propagate, and the policy-FDT deferral only addresses cases with several self-consistent assignments, which isn't the case here. That leaves defection as the A-FDT answer, so I'll go with that.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out how to decide under A-FDT as the essay interprets it, with two copies that see different observations, x from Rowan's lead and y from Sasha's. I'm wondering whether intervening on one copy's decision node would change the other's act, or whether that depends on a subjunctive edge, and I'm checking what the essay's procedure says about the edges from D_x.\n\nI'm checking whether the other prisoner's act actually depends on my decision node, since they compute their response to y and not x. If there's no subjunctive edge, A-FDT just gives a best response, and defection dominates. But section 6.2 raises policy-FDT as a deferral option, and I'm trying to pin down whether that applies here, given the essay's wording about self-consistent assignments.\n\nI'm weighing whether the strict A-FDT reading should win here, since the other copy's act comes from y rather than x, and intervening on D_x alone wouldn't reach it without a subjunctive edge. The alternative is that the two copies' outputs are logically linked by symmetry, but the essay's intervention rule doesn't propagate that link on its own.\n\nI'm noting that the essay explicitly keeps D_R fixed when D_L moves, so the one-input intervention doesn't propagate, and the policy-FDT deferral only addresses cases with several self-consistent assignments, which isn't the case here. That leaves defection as the A-FDT answer, so I'll go with that.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9405,"completion_tokens":1489,"total_tokens":10894,"cost":0.0674,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0674,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.03762,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02978},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":932,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}